olving, responsibility, concentration, but attitude (know-it-all, hot temper) could affect teamwork and pressure.\n\nWe need to pick provisional preference. Which candidate best balances? Long-distance flights require reliability, calm under pressure, decision-making, teamwork, communication, adaptability, technical competence. Attitude issues matter.\n\nCandidate A: strong leadership, anticipation, spatial vision, but show-off and not open to new ideas may hinder crew cooperation and adaptability.\n\nCandidate B: conscientious, stress handling, weather assessment, computer skills, but nasty remarks (bad teamwork) and weak number memory (could affect navigation). Might be problematic.\n\nCandidate C: quick correct decisions, calm, tech understanding, but communication difficulty, egocentric, unwilling to learn further. Communication is crucial for crew.\n\nCandidate D: problem solving, responsibility, concentration, handles unexpected events, but hot temper and know-it-all could cause crew friction under stress.\n\nWhich is best? Possibly Candidate A's technical and leadership strengths outweigh attitude? But show-off and closed to new ideas could be serious for long flights where crew cooperation and adaptability to new procedures matter. Candidate B's nasty remarks also severe. Candidate C's communication issues also severe. Candidate D's hot temper also severe.\n\nWe need to weigh seriousness. Which weakness is most serious? For safety, teamwork and communication are critical. A show-off may be less directly harmful than nasty remarks? But not open to new ideas could hinder adopting new procedures. Hot temper could cause conflict under stress. Egocentric also.\n\nMaybe Candidate A is still best because strengths are directly relevant to flight safety (anticipation, spatial vision, leadership) and negatives are more about attitude but could be managed. Candidate D also strong but hot temper may be dangerous under long stress.\n\nThus provisional preference: Candid